    <h2>Raw materials &amp; sourcing</h2>
    <p>
      Incense begins with the material, and our published sourcing guidance is built around one
      principle: the useful question is <em>what</em> the material is — species, part, and grade — not
      what it is called. The discipline we document in
      <a href="/blog/bulk-incense-ingredient-sourcing/">bulk ingredient sourcing</a> is the same one we
      apply: match the material form to the downstream process (whole, chip, powder, resin), hold
      batch-to-batch consistency, and specify particle size and moisture for the specific formula
      rather than chasing a false universal number.
    </p>
    <p>
      Because botanicals are natural materials, not standardized chemicals, the same name can cover
      real variation in species and grade. A "sandalwood" may be *Santalum album*, another *Santalum*
      species, or a synthetic fragrance; a "borneol" may be a natural crystal, a leaf-distilled
      extract, or a synthetic isomer mixture. Where authenticity risk is material, we point buyers to
      the <a href="/blog/incense-authentication-database/">authentication database</a> and the
      <a href="/blog/incense-substitution-cross-reference/">substitution cross-reference</a> rather
      than making any claim we cannot trace to a species, an origin, or a published page. The
      buyer-side framework — how to vet a supplier, sample, and inspect before shipment — is in the
      <a href="/blog/wholesale-guide/">wholesale guide</a> and the
      <a href="/blog/factory-vetting-guide/">factory vetting guide</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Sampling &amp; formula development</h2>
    <p>
      For OEM and private-label work, the process runs
      <strong>brief → formula direction → sampling → iteration → production sampling → scale-up</strong>.
      We develop against a written brief, produce a development sample, and iterate until the sample
      matches the brief; the approved sample becomes the reference standard every later batch is
      checked against. See
      <a href="/blog/custom-fragrance-development/">custom fragrance development</a> and the
      <a href="/blog/samples-program/">samples program</a> for how this works from the buyer's side.
    </p>
    <p>
      We are explicit about the limit: a sample verifies the <em>product</em> — its aroma, format,
      material, and burn — not the supplier's ability to deliver at volume. A trial order, not a sample,
      is the step that verifies the relationship. The buyer's leverage is precision: the more exactly
      you can name the gap between sample and brief, the fewer iteration rounds it takes to close it.
    </p>

    <h2>Manufacturing capabilities</h2>
    <p>
      We state capability honestly. LanDeng supplies Chinese botanical incense across the formats named
      above and works through the wholesale, OEM, and private-label paths. What we do <em>not</em>
      publish is equally deliberate, and it is a feature of this page, not an omission:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Production capacity, factory size, and line counts:</strong> not publicly specified.
        We do not publish capacity figures, and we will not invent them.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Certifications and test documents:</strong> not publicly asserted. As our
        <a href="/blog/factory-vetting-guide/">factory vetting guide</a> explains, certifications are
        claims to verify per order, not badges to assume.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>MOQ, lead time, and pricing:</strong> confirmed per inquiry. There is no published
        price list, and we state figures only as "on request" for a specific product and quantity.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      This restraint is the point. A manufacturer page that lists a number for every box is usually
      inventing them; we would rather state what is verifiable and mark the rest "not publicly
      specified" than fabricate a credential. A buyer who asks about capability should expect the same
      honesty in return: ask how we check, and we describe the process — ask for a capacity figure we
      have not published, and we will say so rather than guess.
    </p>

