    <h2>Quality control</h2>
    <p>
      The quality-control discipline we document — and apply — is a three-stage process:
      <strong>incoming inspection</strong> (verifying species, grade, and adulteration when material
      arrives), <strong>in-process control</strong> (holding blend, particle size, moisture, and
      forming steady during production), and <strong>final inspection</strong> (checking the finished
      product against the agreed specification before release). The full treatment is in
      <a href="/blog/incense-manufacturing-quality-control/">manufacturing quality control</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      In commercial incense, the product you reorder is the <em>consistency</em>, not the individual
      stick. Our buyer-facing guidance therefore centers on the question every buyer should ask any
      maker: <em>"how do you check, and can you prove the last batch matched the one before?"</em>
      The five variables that matter — material consistency, particle size, moisture, density, and burn
      behavior — are all things a buyer can verify in part on a single careful sample burn. Concretely,
      the questions we encourage a buyer to ask any maker are the same ones we answer: how do you sample
      each batch, what specification do you check against, and what happens when a batch fails. A maker
      who can describe all three is demonstrating a real system; one who can only say "it never fails"
      is not.
    </p>

    <h2>Packaging &amp; documentation</h2>
    <p>
      Packaging does three jobs at once — protect the product, seal the aroma, and satisfy the
      destination's labeling and compliance rules — and we agree bulk-vs-retail, sealing, and
      fragility handling per order. Documentation is tied to each lot: species and origin statements,
      wild-vs-cultivated declarations where relevant, and test documents as agreed. See
      <a href="/blog/packaging-shipping-guide/">packaging &amp; shipping</a> and
      <a href="/blog/incense-export-considerations/">export considerations</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Export &amp; B2B service</h2>
    <p>
      For cross-border orders, the work breaks into compliance documents, species controls (CITES
      applies to botanicals such as agarwood and certain sandalwoods), destination labeling, and
      transport of a combustible botanical. We confirm the specific document set, HS classification,
      and permits per shipment against the destination's authoritative schedule — we do not quote
      memorized codes or duty rates.
    </p>
    <p>
      Everything above is offered through a single, honest inquiry path: we prepare a quotation per
      product, quantity, and destination. See
      <a href="/blog/moq-pricing-guide/">why quotes beat price lists</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>How to start a project</h2>
    <p>
      A useful inquiry states the product in buyer terms, because those variables reshape every
      quotation. To prepare an accurate quote, please tell us:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Format</strong> — stick, cone, coil, sachet, or raw material.</li>
      <li><strong>Material or aroma profile</strong> — a named material, a scent family, or a reference product.</li>
      <li><strong>Target quantity</strong> — even a range is useful; it drives MOQ and unit cost.</li>
      <li><strong>Packaging</strong> — plain bulk or retail-ready, and your label if any.</li>
      <li><strong>Destination</strong> — country and port, which drives shipping and duties.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      We confirm <strong>sampling, production lead time, MOQ, and documentation</strong> per project —
      we do not publish fixed figures because every project differs.
    </p>

    <h2>What we will not claim</h2>
    <p>
      To close on the same note we opened: this page makes no ranking claims (we are not "the best" or
      "the leading" anything), no unverified history, no invented capacity, and no fabricated
      certification. We do not describe incense as a treatment, a sleep aid, or a remedy. What we can
      state — who we are, what we supply, and the sourcing, QC, and export discipline we apply — is
      stated plainly and traceably. The rest is marked "not publicly specified" or "on request," which
      is the honest answer when the number genuinely depends on your inputs.
    </p>

